{
  "analysis": "Key finding: no /dev/kvm (so TCG software emulation, slower), no ISO extraction tools, and mount -o loop is blocked. But qemu, expect, and python3 are present. The alpine.iso is in /app. Rather than fight extraction, the decisive question is whether the Alpine live ISO enables a serial console (ttyS0) by default — if it does, I can boot with -serial and get an auto-login root shell directly, making configuration trivial. Let me test that empirically.",
  "plan": "Boot the ISO in the background with a serial port writing to a log file and the monitor on a unix socket (no KVM, so TCG). Wait and inspect /tmp/serial.log. If Alpine outputs boot messages to ttyS0 and presents a login/shell, serial console works and I can automate the rest with expect. If the log is empty, I'll need to enable serial another way.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "cd /app\n", "duration": 0.1},
    {"keystrokes": "nohup qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512 -smp 2 -cdrom /app/alpine.iso -netdev user,id=n0,hostfwd=tcp::2222-:22 -device e1000,netdev=n0 -display none -serial file:/tmp/serial.log -monitor unix:/tmp/qmon.sock,server,nowait >/tmp/qemu.out 2>&1 &\n", "duration": 1.0},
    {"keystrokes": "sleep 8; echo '--- qemu.out ---'; cat /tmp/qemu.out; echo '--- serial.log ---'; cat /tmp/serial.log\n", "duration": 9.0}
  ]
}

The serial console is empty — the Alpine live ISO does not enable ttyS0 by default. I need to make the kernel boot with `console=ttyS0` (Alpine's inittab already spawns a getty on ttyS0). To do that without the CD's bootloader, I must extract the kernel + initrd + boot config from the ISO. Let me check whether I can install an extraction tool or have network.

{
  "analysis": "QEMU booted but /tmp/serial.log is empty, confirming the Alpine live ISO does NOT enable a serial console by default. No /dev/kvm (TCG emulation). To get scriptable access I'll boot the extracted kernel with 'console=ttyS0' appended — Alpine's inittab already spawns a getty on ttyS0, so root (no password) will be reachable. I need to extract kernel, initramfs, and the syslinux append line from the ISO. No mount, no 7z/xorriso present. Let me test whether apt/network is available to install xorriso, otherwise I'll extract via Python.",
  "plan": "Test network/apt availability. If reachable, install xorriso (provides osirrox) to extract the ISO. If not, I'll write a minimal Python ISO9660 parser. Checking now.",
  "commands": [
    {"keystrokes": "apt-get --version 2>&1 | head -1; echo ---; timeout 8 apt-get install -y xorriso 2>&1 | tail -15\n", "duration": 12.0}
  ]
